            "text": "{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"scope\",\n      \"location\": \"(x : Fin n → ℝ)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The variables x are fixed before N, c, and a are chosen. The informal phrase “expressed identically” calls for one choice of N, c, and a that works for every x. The formal proposition instead permits those choices to depend on x.\",\n      \"prediction\": \"The comparison proposition places x after the existential choices. Reviewing the quantifier order should expose the translation difference; proving equivalence would not by itself resolve it, since both propositions may be true.\",\n      \"probe_kind\": \"equivalence\",\n      \"comparison\": \"∀ (n : ℕ), n > 0 → ∃ (N : ℕ) (c : Fin N → ℚ) (a : ℕ → Fin n → ℝ), (∀ (i : Fin N) (j : Fin n), a i j = -1 ∨ a i j = 0 ∨ a i j = 1) ∧ ∀ (x : Fin n → ℝ), (∏ i : Fin n, x i) = ∑ i : Fin N, c i * (∑ j : Fin n, a i j * x j) ^ n\",\n      \"bridge\": \"translated\",\n      \"obligation\": \"Review whether “identically” requires coefficients and N independent of x. Logical equivalence between the two true theorems would not establish that the original quantifier order faithfully translates that requirement.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The formal statement appears to make an identical polynomial representation pointwise in x rather than choosing one representation for all x.\"\n}"
